WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.976
On a surface with two handles, how far can one
twist move a loop?

2
00:00:04.367 --> 00:00:06.033
A whole loop is one vertex.

3
00:00:06.203 --> 00:00:09.913
Two vertices share an edge if their loops can be
drawn disjointly.

4
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:12.517
Distance counts the fewest of these moves.

5
00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:14.644
Drawing six edges leaves a question.

6
00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:15.915
Could five have worked?

7
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:21.552
Start with two loops, A and B, that cross twelve
times and sit four edges apart.

8
00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:25.350
To twist A around B, open a thin collar into a
strip.

9
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.203
Its left and right sides match.

10
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.670
Keep the bottom still and slide the top one full
turn.

11
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.902
Watch a strand leave on the right and reappear on
the left.

12
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.952
Each of twelve moving strands crosses each of
twelve fixed strands.

13
00:00:38.122 --> 00:00:41.053
One twist gives a hundred and forty-four
crossings.

14
00:00:41.223 --> 00:00:42.986
Two give two hundred and eighty-eight.

15
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.745
Mahanta proved that one twist of this particular
pair gives distance five.

16
00:00:47.915 --> 00:00:49.382
What happens at two twists?

17
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:53.237
Take a shortest path of four edges from A to B.

18
00:00:53.407 --> 00:00:55.509
Stop one step early, at W.

19
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.236
W misses B, so twisting around B fixes W.

20
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:03.594
Copy the first three edges, twist the copy, and
attach it in reverse.

21
00:01:03.764 --> 00:01:05.797
Three edges out, three edges back.

22
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.239
This gives six moves to the twisted loop.

23
00:01:08.409 --> 00:01:11.577
To prove the distance, we have to rule out every
shortcut.

24
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:13.936
Try four twists first.

25
00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:17.678
Imagine lifting the path into an annular cover
around B.

26
00:01:17.848 --> 00:01:21.953
When every loop crosses B, each edge can advance
at most one step there.

27
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:25.402
Four twists put the projected ends six steps
apart.

28
00:01:25.572 --> 00:01:27.495
Five edges cannot span that gap.

29
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:33.410
And if the path visits a loop that misses B,
reaching that loop costs at least three moves
from either end.

30
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.464
Again, six.

31
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:36.877
Now turn the dial down to two twists.

32
00:01:37.047 --> 00:01:38.848
The projected gap falls to four.

33
00:01:39.018 --> 00:01:42.266
A five-edge shortcut is still possible on this
evidence.

34
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.392
Here is how to finish.

35
00:01:44.562 --> 00:01:46.566
Cut along A, and triangulate.

36
00:01:46.736 --> 00:01:51.469
The crossing counts on the sides of a triangle
tell us how the strands connect inside.

37
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:55.466
An efficient-geodesic theorem says that if a
shortcut exists,

38
00:01:55.561 --> 00:01:58.210
a shortest one starts with bounded crossing
counts.

39
00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.960
We can test a finite list.

40
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.520
For the double twist, there are eighty candidate
first moves.

41
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.855
Following them produces fourteen thousand and
sixteen terminal tests.

42
00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:10.937
Every terminal pair fills the surface with disks.

43
00:02:11.107 --> 00:02:12.841
No essential loop misses both.

44
00:02:13.011 --> 00:02:14.792
Every proposed shortcut fails.

45
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.376
So the answer for this drawing is exact.

46
00:02:17.546 --> 00:02:18.919
One twist gives five.

47
00:02:19.089 --> 00:02:22.535
Two twists, three twists, and every higher power
give six.

48
00:02:23.467 --> 00:02:26.136
Could a single twist ever get all the way to six?

49
00:02:26.306 --> 00:02:29.130
Change the starting pair, but keep its distance
at four.

50
00:02:29.533 --> 00:02:32.597
Twelve crossings is the minimum on the double
torus.

51
00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:35.687
There are five kinds of ordered starting pairs at
that size.

52
00:02:35.857 --> 00:02:37.606
Every one twists to distance five.

53
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.542
At thirteen crossings there are eleven kinds.

54
00:02:40.712 --> 00:02:42.579
Again, every one twists to five.

55
00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:46.047
So a pair can give five without having minimum
intersection.

56
00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:49.672
At fourteen crossings, one pair finally reaches
six.

57
00:02:49.842 --> 00:02:53.319
The two resulting curves cross a hundred and
ninety-six times.

58
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:58.702
Fourteen is therefore the smallest starting count
that works for this one-twist construction.

59
00:02:58.872 --> 00:03:02.426
The minimum over all distance-six pairs is a
separate question.

60
00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:06.947
Curves at every distance were known long before
this calculation.

61
00:03:07.117 --> 00:03:09.171
Here is how the general idea works.

62
00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:13.640
With enough twists, fold a length-four geodesic
into one of length six.

63
00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.662
Fold again and its length is ten.

64
00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:17.880
Stop seven edges along that path.

65
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:20.828
Any shortcut to that point would shorten the
whole path.

66
00:03:20.998 --> 00:03:23.886
So those two vertices are exactly seven apart.

67
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.383
The short-twist result answers a more precise
question.

68
00:03:27.553 --> 00:03:29.903
For Mahanta's drawing, two turns suffice.

69
00:03:30.073 --> 00:03:33.378
With the right fourteen-crossing starting pair,
one turn does it.
